<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf Hitler's rise to power</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hitler's rise to power</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s rise to power began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> in September 1919, when he joined the German Workers' Party, soon renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, which he led from 1921. After the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> of 1923 and nine months in prison, where he wrote Mein Kampf, he sought power by legal means on a platform of anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, antisemitism and ultranationalism. In July 1932, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, the Nazis became the largest party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> appointed Hitler chancellor on 30 January 1933; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> and the Enabling Act gave him dictatorial powers, and in August 1934 he merged the chancellery and the presidency as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-hitlers-rise-to-power.jpg" alt="Adolf Hitler's rise to power" width="330" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitler_as_young_man.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Hitler, Austrian-born, had served in a Bavarian regiment through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in November 1918, during a period of socialist upheaval and political murders. After the Bavarian Soviet Republic was crushed in May 1919 he gave evidence against fellow soldiers, and his anti-communist views kept him in the army. In the German Workers' Party he became chief of propaganda in early 1920 with the support of Anton Drexler. On 24 February 1920, before some 2,000 people at the Hofbräuhaus in Munich, he announced the party's 25-point programme. A hall guard formed in 1920 became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturmabteilung">Sturmabteilung</a> (SA) by November 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s March on Rome, Hitler tried to seize power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> of 8–9 November 1923. Nearly 2,000 party members marched to the Marienplatz, where a police cordon met them; sixteen Nazis and four police officers were killed. Arrested on 11 November and tried from February 1924, he used the court to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, and on 1 April was sentenced to five years at Landsberg Prison, where he was treated well.</p><p>In the federal election of May 1928 the party won only 12 seats and 2.6% of the vote, while the SA fought the communist Roter Frontkämpferbund in the streets. After the Wall Street crash the Nazis and Communists together won almost 40% of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> seats in 1930. Hitler, who had given up his Austrian citizenship in 1925, became a German citizen only through an appointment in the Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. In the presidential run-off of 10 April 1932 he won 36.8% against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a>'s 53.0%.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire of February 1933, for which the Nazis blamed the communists after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> communist Marinus van der Lubbe was found at the scene, the Communists were excluded from the Reichstag. On 23 March 1933, with Nazi paramilitaries around the building, the Centre Party and the conservatives voted for the Enabling Act; only the Social Democrats voted against. All other parties were outlawed on 14 July 1933. The historian Alan Bullock held that the heaviest responsibility lay with the German right, which made Hitler its partner in government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolf Hitler's rise to power” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its emergency powers were later exploited in <b>Hitler's rise to power</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
